 # Toomey Syringe
 
-The **Toomey syringe** (catheter-tip syringe) is the **manual bladder-irrigation workhorse** — a wide-barrel, **60 mL** syringe with a **tapered open catheter tip** (no Luer hub) that seats directly into the funnel end of a Foley, Coudé, or three-way catheter. It is the standard bedside, OR back-table, and inpatient-ward instrument for **manual bladder washout (MBW), tissue / clot evacuation through a catheter, and intravesical medication instillation**.
+A **Toomey-tip syringe** is a large-volume irrigation syringe with a broad tapered outlet. It is used when its tip matches the catheter, tubing, or instrument interface specified by the product and local protocol. “Toomey tip” and “catheter tip” are related clinical descriptions but are not a guarantee that every syringe fits every catheter.
 
-## Design
+## Current product example
 
-- **60 mL barrel** (50 / 70 mL variants exist; 60 mL is the predominant clinical size).
-- **Tapered smooth catheter tip** — slips directly into the catheter funnel with a friction seal; no Luer adapter required.
-- **Wide internal bore** — accepts small clot fragments, mucus, and tissue chips on aspiration without clogging.
-- **Flanged plunger** — supports one-handed thumb-and-finger irrigation-aspiration cycles.
-- **Disposable polypropylene**; reusable autoclavable glass variants are historical.
+The current US device record for Monoject model **8881560265** identifies it as a **60 mL syringe with Toomey tip**. The record lists it as prescription-only, sterile-packaged, single-use, and not made with natural-rubber latex.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup> Those attributes are model-specific: verify capacity, tip geometry, sterility, latex status, and approved use on the package rather than treating them as universal properties of the device name.
 
-## Reconstructive / Functional Urology Uses
+## Manual bladder washout
 
-### Manual Bladder Washout for Clot Retention
+Manual washout is a clinical procedure, not simply a syringe maneuver. Current New South Wales guidance describes irrigation through a two- or three-way catheter with sterile 0.9% sodium chloride and a catheter-tip syringe, using aseptic technique and **gentle** pressure. It warns that excessive pressure can disrupt sutures or cause bladder perforation and extravasation and calls for urology oversight, particularly after recent urologic surgery.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
 
-The dominant use. The Toomey is the irrigation arm of the **CATCH-22 protocol** (Clarebrough 2018) — a structured manual-bladder-washout pathway for inpatient clot retention. The protocol pairs a Toomey-type catheter-tip syringe with a **≥ 22 Fr catheter** and emphasizes **aggressive irrigation volume**: the urology service performed washouts averaging **~ 5,400 mL** per attempt vs **~ 145 mL** for referring teams — a ~ 37× volume gap that explained most of the clinical failures referred to urology.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
+The 2024 European Association of Urology Nurses guideline gives a 60 mL syringe and sterile saline as an equipment example and similarly advises slow, even pressure, never forcing against resistance, and strict fluid input/output accounting. Frail or irradiated bladders warrant particular caution. The guideline also notes that evidence is inadequate to define an optimal washout solution, timing, or frequency.<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
 
-Technique: **instill 50–60 mL normal saline → aspirate**, repeat until the return is clear. **Forceful but controlled** — over-pressurization in a small-capacity / trabeculated / neurogenic bladder risks bladder perforation; under-volume irrigation just moves clot around.
+:::danger[Stop rather than force]
+Pain, marked resistance, inability to recover irrigant, suspected catheter malposition, new abdominal distension, or concern for recent repair or perforation requires reassessment and escalation. Do not convert resistance into higher hand pressure.
+:::
 
-The companion catheter physics (lumen geometry, eyelet pattern, three-way vs two-way) are addressed on the [Three-Way Catheter](/docs/foundations/tools/biomaterials/urinary-catheters/three-way-catheter) page.
+### Evidence behind the CATCH-22 protocol
 
-### Post-Procedure Bladder Irrigation
+CATCH-22 was a **prospective service study**, not a randomized trial. Patients referred with clot retention commonly had 16–20 Fr catheters and had received a mean washout volume of 145 mL, whereas the urology service used a mean of 5,392 mL and recovered a mean clot volume of 617 mL. The resulting protocol emphasized infection control, catheter manipulation, at least a 22 Fr catheter, removing visible clot and then irrigating an additional litre, and seeking help when clearance was not achieved.<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup>
 
-After [TURP](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/endoscopy/resectoscope) / [HoLEP](/docs/surgical-techniques/bph-male-luts/prostate-enucleation-holep) chip evacuation by [Ellik](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/suction/ellik-evacuator), after open bladder closure, after [AUS](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments) or [IPP](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments) reservoir / pump work that disturbs the bladder, and after any urethroplasty or fistula-repair case with intravesical work — a final Toomey washout through the catheter at the end of the case clears residual blood, chip remnants, and irrigation debris before the patient leaves the OR.
+These observations support a structured, adequately resourced washout pathway; they do not prove that one syringe design or a fixed force is superior. Catheter choice remains important. Laboratory studies show that drainage lumen, eyelet, and catheter geometry materially affect manual washout performance, but these are bench comparisons rather than patient trials.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)[[6]](#ref6)[[7]](#ref7)</sup>
 
-### Intravesical Medication Instillation
+## Practical workflow boundaries
 
-The Toomey is the standard instillation device for non-oncologic bladder agents in the reconstructive / functional urology space:
+- Confirm the order, indication, catheter type, recent operation, and local protocol before irrigation.
+- Use the sterile solution, equipment, volume increments, and aseptic method specified by that protocol.
+- Apply slow, even pressure; do not force an obstructed system.
+- Account for instilled and recovered volume and document clot, urine characteristics, patient tolerance, catheter patency, and escalation.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)[[3]](#ref3)</sup>
+- After recent TURP, radical prostatectomy, bladder resection, cystotomy, reconstruction, or another fresh urinary repair, involve the operating urology team rather than applying a generic bedside recipe.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
 
-- **Povidone-iodine bladder irrigation** for recurrent UTI prevention in NLUTD patients on CIC — Moussa 2021 RCT data support reduction in symptomatic rUTI with periodic dilute-povidone instillation.<sup>[[2]](#ref2)</sup>
-- **Heparin / DMSO / lidocaine bladder cocktails** for [IC / BPS](/docs/clinical-conditions) symptomatic relief.
-- **Intravesical antibiotic instillation** in selected NLUTD / catheterized patients per institutional protocol.
-- **Bladder priming for urodynamics or VCUG** when a gravity drip is not set up — barrel as funnel with the plunger removed.
+The device record supports use as an irrigation syringe; it does not establish a universal protocol for intravesical drugs, urodynamic filling, wound irrigation, or catheter-position diagnosis. Those uses require the relevant drug/device instructions and specialty protocol.
 
-(Intravesical chemotherapy and BCG instillation for bladder cancer is the oncology use case, outside WARWIKI scope.)
+## When manual washout does not clear the bladder
 
-### Catheter Patency / Troubleshooting
+Persistent clot retention may require catheter exchange, endoscopic evacuation, hemostasis, or another operative strategy. [Ellik evacuation](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/suction/ellik-evacuator) is a sheath-based operative workflow, not a higher-force version of bedside syringe irrigation.
 
-The Toomey is the first-line bedside tool for the **"is this Foley actually obstructed?"** question — a gentle 30–60 mL instill / aspirate cycle distinguishes mechanical obstruction (no return, no flow restoration) from clot occlusion (return clears with washout) from balloon malposition (asymmetric flow).
+Selected reports describe direct sheath suction, chymotrypsin/sodium-bicarbonate treatment, or hydrogen-peroxide irrigation after conventional evacuation failed.<sup>[[8]](#ref8)[[9]](#ref9)[[10]](#ref10)</sup> These are limited salvage reports and do not establish routine bedside regimens. Povidone-iodine bladder irrigation has also been reported in a prospective before-and-after cohort of patients with neurogenic lower urinary tract dysfunction using intermittent catheterization; it was **not** a randomized trial and should not be generalized into a device-level recommendation.<sup>[[11]](#ref11)</sup>
 
-### Bedside / OR-Adjacent Adjunct Uses
+## Device comparison
 
-- **Wound and graft irrigation** — buccal-graft bed, donor-site irrigation, perineal-wound copious-irrigation pre-closure.
-- **Donor-site rinse during STSG** harvest with the irrigant of choice.
-- **Bedside cystoscopy bladder fill** when the dedicated tubing is unavailable — barrel-as-funnel.
-
-## When Toomey Fails — Adjuncts for Tenacious Clot Retention
-
-The Toomey is rate-limited by the operator's hand and the catheter calibre. When standard MBW does not clear the clot:
-
-| Adjunct | Mechanism | Evidence |
+| Device | Interface | Typical role |
 |---|---|---|
-| **Direct sheath-to-wall-suction (~ 250 mmHg)** via cystoscopy | Higher negative pressure than any manual technique | Goel 2011 case series — successful in patients who failed Ellik / Toomey MBW |
-| **Chymotrypsin + sodium bicarbonate intravesical irrigation** | Enzymatic clot dissolution | Bo 2014 non-surgical-treatment series<sup>[[3]](#ref3)</sup> |
-| **Hydrogen peroxide bladder irrigation** | Oxidative clot dissolution | Xu 2020 case series — simple, effective in case-series data<sup>[[4]](#ref4)</sup> |
-| **OR cystoscopy with [Ellik](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/suction/ellik-evacuator) evacuation** | Larger-bore sheath, mechanical chip retrieval | Standard escalation when bedside MBW fails |
-| **Open / robotic cystotomy with clot evacuation** | Direct visualization | Last-line salvage; reserved for surgical bladder or persistent uncontrollable retention |
-
-## Toomey vs Standard Syringes vs Bulb Syringe
-
-| Device | Capacity | Catheter interface | Best fit |
-|---|---|---|---|
-| **Toomey syringe** | **60 mL** (50 / 70 variants) | **Tapered open tip → catheter funnel** | Bladder irrigation, MBW, intravesical instillation |
-| **Luer-tip 60 mL syringe** | 60 mL | Luer-lock → adapter required | General irrigation; not catheter-funnel compatible |
-| **Asepto bulb syringe** | ~ 60–120 mL | Open tapered tip | Wound irrigation, cavity rinsing; less controlled vacuum on aspiration |
-| **Ellik evacuator** | Continuous-cycle bulb | Sheath-mounted | Chip / clot evacuation **through resectoscope sheath** during transurethral cases |
-
-## Catheter Pairing for Maximum Flow
+| **Toomey-tip syringe** | Broad tapered outlet; confirm fit | Controlled manual irrigation through a compatible interface |
+| **Catheter-tip syringe** | Tapered catheter interface; size varies | Manual catheter irrigation under protocol |
+| **Luer syringe** | Luer slip/lock | Luer-compatible access; not interchangeable with a catheter funnel without the intended adapter |
+| **[Ellik evacuator](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/suction/ellik-evacuator)** | Compatible endoscopic sheath | Operative tissue, fragment, or clot evacuation |
 
-Effective MBW depends as much on the catheter as the syringe. Mesfin 2011 and Kobatake 2022 both quantified the design dependence: **larger-lumen, multi-eyelet, three-way irrigation catheters** clear clot substantially faster than smaller / single-eyelet variants.<sup>[[5]](#ref5)[[6]](#ref6)</sup> Braasch 2006 mapped the lumen-geometry trade-offs across three-way designs.<sup>[[7]](#ref7)</sup> See the [Three-Way Catheter](/docs/foundations/tools/biomaterials/urinary-catheters/three-way-catheter) page for the catheter side of the pairing.
+See also: [Three-Way Catheter](/docs/foundations/tools/biomaterials/urinary-catheters/three-way-catheter) and [Rigid Cystoscope](/docs/foundations/tools/instruments/endoscopy/rigid-cystoscope).
 
-CATCH-22 reinforces this: **≥ 22 Fr** is the practical minimum for adult clot retention; a Toomey paired with a 16 Fr Foley will lose the case on flow physics alone.<sup>[[1]](#ref1)</sup>
-
-## Limitations
-
-- **Operator-power-limited** — the hand-driven pressure ceiling is far below wall-suction or true cystoscopic evacuation.
-- **Trabeculated / small-capacity / NLUTD bladder** — over-vigorous instillation risks **bladder perforation**; instill smaller volumes (20–30 mL) and aspirate more frequently in this group.
-- **Recent bladder anastomosis or fresh cystotomy closure** — relatively contraindicated for forceful MBW until surgeon-set day-X; gentle low-volume irrigation only.
-- **Friction-seal slip** — the tapered tip occasionally backs out under pressure; brace the catheter funnel with the non-dominant hand.
+---
 
-## Historical Context — David Toomey
+## References
 
-The Toomey syringe is named after **Dr. David Toomey**, who designed it as a purpose-built catheter-interface syringe for efficient bladder irrigation. The simple, purpose-built design has remained the global standard for manual bladder irrigation and intravesical instillation across nearly a century of urologic practice.
